        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Roberto Fellini",
        "type": "Person",
        "description": "Filmmaker who directed 'Prova d’orchestra', a film cited to illustrate the concept of orchestration in hierarchical systems.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:24:28.550Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Rudolf Clausius",
        "type": "Person",
        "description": "Physicist who introduced the concept of entropy in the 1850s as a measure of disorder or randomness in a system.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:25:01.228Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Sadi Carnot",
        "type": "Person",
        "description": "Physicist whose seminal work on thermodynamics laid the foundation for Rudolf Clausius's definition of entropy.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:25:01.366Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Ludwig Boltzmann",
        "type": "Person",
        "description": "Physicist who defined entropy in statistical physics during the 1870s as a measure of the number of possible microscopic configurations consistent with a macroscopic state.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:25:01.501Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Statistical Physics",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A branch of physics that emerged in the 1870s, using statistical methods to explain the thermodynamic behavior of systems, notably through Boltzmann's work on entropy.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:25:07.843Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Microstates",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "The specific microscopic configurations of a system that are consistent with a given macroscopic state, used in Boltzmann's definition of entropy.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:25:12.576Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Claude Shannon",
        "type": "Person",
        "description": "Mathematician and electrical engineer who introduced the concept of information entropy in 1948, linking thermodynamic entropy to the randomness or uncertainty of information in a message.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:25:29.799Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Information Entropy",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A measure introduced by Claude Shannon that quantifies the randomness, uncertainty, or information content of a message, where higher uncertainty corresponds to higher information content.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:25:29.958Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Mutual Information",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A measure derived from information entropy that quantifies the statistical dependence between two random variables, calculated as the sum of individual entropies minus the joint entropy.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:25:30.088Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Decision-making",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A cognitive task involving the selection of a course of action among several alternatives, analyzed within the Thermodynamics of Mind framework to understand hierarchical brain organization.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:26:15.748Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Anaesthesia",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A drug-induced state characterized by loss of consciousness and sensation, used in the Thermodynamics of Mind framework to analyze changes in brain hierarchy and irreversibility.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:26:20.425Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Psychedelics",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A class of psychoactive drugs that alter perception and cognition, studied within the Thermodynamics of Mind framework to reveal differences in brain state hierarchy compared to normal wakefulness.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:26:20.560Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Coma",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A prolonged state of unconsciousness often resulting from illness or injury, analyzed in the Thermodynamics of Mind framework to understand extreme deviations in brain hierarchical organization.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:26:20.688Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Neuropsychiatric Disorders",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A broad category of mental health conditions with neurological bases, investigated using the Thermodynamics of Mind framework to identify underlying mechanisms of altered brain dynamics.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:26:20.815Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Equilibrium",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A thermodynamic state where a system is stable and reversible in time, associated with flat, non-hierarchical organization in the context of brain dynamics.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:26:20.946Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Nonequilibrium",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A thermodynamic state where a system is dynamic and irreversible in time, necessarily present in hierarchical systems such as the brain.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:26:25.698Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Forward Time Series",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "The natural chronological ordering of brain signal data, used in thermodynamic analysis to detect the arrow of time and irreversibility by comparing it against backward time series.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:27:14.882Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Backward Time Series",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "Artificially reverted time ordering of brain signal data, used in thermodynamic analysis to detect the arrow of time and irreversibility by contrasting it with forward time series.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:27:15.035Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Flat Hierarchy",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A brain organizational state characterized by uniform symmetric information flow and full reversibility, representing the absence of hierarchical structure in the thermodynamic framework.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:27:15.163Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Model-free Approach",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A method of analyzing temporal whole-brain data that does not rely on pre-defined models, instead using thermodynamic principles like irreversibility to quantify hierarchy directly from the data.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:27:15.293Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Causal Mechanistic Generators",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "The underlying causes and mechanisms of functional hierarchy in the brain, identified through whole-brain modelling that integrates anatomical connectivity with functional dynamics.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:27:15.425Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Stuart–Landau oscillators",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A type of nonlinear oscillator model used in whole-brain modeling to fit empirical irreversibility and simulate brain dynamics.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:28:31.159Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Generative Effective Connectivity",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A matrix derived from fitting a generative whole-brain model to irreversibility data, revealing the causal, mechanistic generators of functional hierarchy in a specific brain state.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:28:31.309Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Generative Hierarchy",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "The hierarchical structure of brain states arising from underlying generative mechanisms, characterized by properties such as trophic coherence, cycle structure, stability, and percolation.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:28:31.439Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Trophic Coherence",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A measure used to characterize generative hierarchy, capturing aspects of cycle structure, stability, and percolation within the brain's network organization.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:28:31.568Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Temporal Evolution NETwork",
        "type": "Tool",
        "description": "A machine learning implementation (TENET) used to quantify irreversibility in fMRI data, revealing differences in brain hierarchy between resting states and cognitive tasks.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:28:31.700Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Resting State",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A baseline brain state characterized by significantly lower irreversibility and a flatter hierarchy compared to active cognitive tasks, as identified by thermodynamic analysis.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:28:31.827Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Cognitive Tasks",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "Active mental processes that, when performed, exhibit higher levels of irreversibility and deeper hierarchical organization compared to the resting state.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:28:31.958Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "INSIDEOUT",
        "type": "Tool",
        "description": "A computational implementation developed to capture the balance between intrinsic (INSIDE) and extrinsic (OUT) brain dynamics. It estimates the arrow of time in brain signals using time-shifted correlation matrices to quantify irreversibility and nonequilibrium, offering a more computationally efficient alternative to TENET that works with smaller datasets.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:29:27.426Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Attention Deficit-Hyperactivity Disorder",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A neuropsychiatric disorder characterized by attention deficits and hyperactivity, analyzed using thermodynamic frameworks like TENET which revealed lower irreversibility during resting states compared to controls.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:29:34.407Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Bipolar Disorder",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A neuropsychiatric disorder involving mood swings, studied via thermodynamic analysis of fMRI data showing reduced irreversibility in resting states, indicating issues with brain orchestration.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:29:34.546Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Schizophrenia",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A severe mental disorder affecting thinking, feeling, and behavior, identified in thermodynamic studies as having lower irreversibility during resting states, suggesting disrupted hierarchical brain organization.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:29:34.682Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Time-Shifted Correlation Matrices",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "A mathematical tool used in the INSIDEOUT framework to quantify irreversibility by analyzing the asymmetry in correlations between brain signals at different time shifts, providing a signature of the arrow of time.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:29:34.821Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Electrocorticography",
        "type": "Tool",
        "description": "A neuroimaging technique that records electrical activity directly from the surface of the brain, often used in non-human primate studies. It generates smaller datasets where INSIDEOUT is particularly effective compared to TENET.",
        "tags": [],
        "created": "2026-05-29T23:29:34.970Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "name": "Non-Human Primates",
        "type": "Concept",
        "description": "Animal subjects used in neuroscience research to study brain states such as awake, deep sleep, and anaesthesia, allowing for the application of tools like INSIDEOUT on electrocorticography data.",
        "tags": [],
