        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "content": "I (agent) note the contrast between the 'minimal computation' of film watching and the 'introspection' of resting state as a key therapeutic insight. If resting is thermodynamically costly and subjectively unpleasant due to mind-wandering, then structured external stimuli like films may serve as a regulatory tool to stabilize brain dynamics, offering a controlled low-irreversibility state that is more restorative than unstructured rest.",
        "title": "Agent observation on films as regulatory tools for brain dynamics",
        "timestamp": "2026-05-29T23:31:47.000Z",
        "source": "thermodynamics-of-mind.pdf"
      },
      {
        "content": "Results from the whole-brain modeling of movie watching confirmed that cognitive tasks exhibit the highest irreversibility, while resting state shows significantly lower irreversibility. Crucially, naturalistic movie watching resulted in significantly lower irreversibility than either the resting state or the cognitive tasks, positioning it as a distinct thermodynamic state.\n\nResults from the whole-brain modeling of movie watching, including analysis of a large HCP dataset, confirmed that cognitive tasks exhibit the highest irreversibility and steeper hierarchy, while resting state shows significantly lower irreversibility and a flatter hierarchy. Crucially, naturalistic movie watching resulted in significantly lower irreversibility than either the resting state or the cognitive tasks, positioning it as a distinct thermodynamic state. This modeling within the Thermodynamics of Mind framework addresses why the subjective experience of watching naturalistic, multimodal films is perceived as more pleasant, motivating, and soothing than both mind-wandering rest and difficult cognitive tasks, providing causal attribution for these subjective differences by linking them to specific thermodynamic states of brain hierarchy.\n\nResults from the whole-brain modeling of movie watching, including analysis of a large HCP dataset, confirmed that cognitive tasks exhibit the highest irreversibility and steeper hierarchy, while resting state shows significantly lower irreversibility and a flatter hierarchy. Crucially, naturalistic movie watching resulted in significantly lower irreversibility than either the resting state or the cognitive tasks, positioning it as a distinct thermodynamic state. \n\nThe whole-brain model captured the mechanistic drivers of these hierarchy changes through generative effective connectivity (GEC), which provides underlying causal mechanisms. Analysis of GEC matrices reveals that the prefrontal cortex is the primary driver for orchestrating computation in the brain (with contributions from parietal, visual, and temporal regions) during the intersection of contrasts between naturalistic film versus rest and rest versus tasks. \n\nThis modeling within the Thermodynamics of Mind framework addresses why the subjective experience of watching naturalistic, multimodal films is perceived as more pleasant, motivating, and soothing than both mind-wandering rest and difficult cognitive tasks. The flatter hierarchy during movie watching is linked to it being a relaxing pastime, offering respite from the thermodynamic demands of survival. Unlike resting, which often involves mind-wandering and introspection that rarely lead to a happy mind, movie watching provides a desirable audio-visual narrative requiring minimal computation, thus providing causal attribution for these subjective differences by linking them to specific thermodynamic states of brain hierarchy.",
