      },
      {
        "content": "The Fluctuation–dissipation theorem (FDT) is defined in the glossary as a central theorem in statistical thermodynamics. It is used for predicting the nonequilibrium fluctuations of a system, specifically relating to the irreversible dissipation of energy into heat from its reversible fluctuations in thermal equilibrium.\n\nThe Fluctuation–dissipation theorem (FDT) is defined in the glossary as a central theorem in statistical thermodynamics. It is used for predicting the nonequilibrium fluctuations of a system, specifically relating to the irreversible dissipation of energy into heat from its reversible fluctuations in thermal equilibrium. Additionally, FDT is identified as a candidate thermodynamic concept for quantifying brain hierarchy, describing the balancing forces of dissipation and spontaneous fluctuations, offering a potential metric for analyzing the thermodynamic state of neural systems.\n\nThe Fluctuation–dissipation theorem (FDT) is defined in the glossary as a central theorem in statistical thermodynamics. It is used for predicting the nonequilibrium fluctuations of a system, specifically relating to the irreversible dissipation of energy into heat from its reversible fluctuations in thermal equilibrium. Additionally, FDT is identified as a candidate thermodynamic concept for quantifying brain hierarchy, describing the balancing forces of dissipation and spontaneous fluctuations, offering a potential metric for analyzing the thermodynamic state of neural systems. The Agent interprets the introduction of FDT as a move toward more granular thermodynamic metrics. While previous contexts focused on turbulence and irreversibility, FDT offers a specific lens on the balance between energy loss (dissipation) and random noise (fluctuations), which is critical for understanding stability in far-from-equilibrium systems like the brain.\n\nThe Fluctuation–dissipation theorem (FDT) is defined in the glossary as a central theorem in statistical thermodynamics. It is used for predicting the nonequilibrium fluctuations of a system, specifically relating to the irreversible dissipation of energy into heat from its reversible fluctuations in thermal equilibrium. The FDT is described as the balancing of friction and thermal noise, or the balancing forces of dissipation and spontaneous fluctuations. This concept lies at the heart of Einstein’s theory of Brownian motion and has been highly successful in describing physical systems in both equilibrium and nonequilibrium states. Additionally, FDT is identified as a candidate thermodynamic concept for quantifying brain hierarchy, offering a potential metric for analyzing the thermodynamic state of neural systems. The Agent interprets the introduction of FDT as a move toward more granular thermodynamic metrics. While previous contexts focused on turbulence and irreversibility, FDT offers a specific lens on the balance between energy loss (dissipation) and random noise (fluctuations), which is critical for understanding stability in far-from-equilibrium systems like the brain.\n\nThe Fluctuation–dissipation theorem (FDT) is defined in the glossary as a central theorem in statistical thermodynamics. It is used for predicting the nonequilibrium fluctuations of a system, specifically relating to the irreversible dissipation of energy into heat from its reversible fluctuations in thermal equilibrium. Additionally, FDT is identified as a candidate thermodynamic concept for quantifying brain hierarchy, describing the balancing forces of dissipation and spontaneous fluctuations, offering a potential metric for analyzing the thermodynamic state of neural systems. The Agent interprets the introduction of FDT as a move toward more granular thermodynamic metrics. While previous contexts focused on turbulence and irreversibility, FDT offers a specific lens on the balance between energy loss (dissipation) and random noise (fluctuations), which is critical for understanding stability in far-from-equilibrium systems like the brain. Specifically, the level of nonequilibrium in brain states can be characterized by computing the violation of the Fluctuation-Dissipation Theorem (FDT). This metric serves as a quantitative measure of how far the brain is from thermodynamic equilibrium, offering a new lens for analyzing states like deep sleep or intense cognitive processing.",
