        "content": "The text suggests implementing a model of the Fluctuation–Dissipation Theorem (FDT) to directly combine model-free and model-based approaches. This integration would use perturbation to reveal the state of nonequilibrium in the brain, serving as a crucial instantiation of the Thermodynamics of Mind framework.\n\nThe text suggests implementing a generative perturbative whole-brain model based on the Fluctuation–Dissipation Theorem (FDT) to estimate FDT violation in empirical neuroimaging data across different brain states (e.g., wakefulness, cognitive tasks, deep sleep). This model-based approach goes beyond model-free analysis of unperturbed states by using Onsager’s regression principle to derive FDT, treating system evolution from perturbation as spontaneous equilibrium fluctuation. It allows characterizing nonequilibrium levels by computing FDT violation. The framework predicts that different pharmacological treatments for depression will lead to distinct hierarchical reconfigurations, explaining varying efficacy and side effects. Future research aims to apply these thermodynamics-based models to non-human animals under anaesthesia and to better understand neuropsychiatric diseases. This serves as a crucial instantiation of the Thermodynamics of Mind framework, combining model-free and model-based approaches to reveal nonequilibrium states and hierarchical organization.",
