      },
      {
        "content": "Kringelbach, M.L. et al. (2023) published research in Science Advances on the mechanisms underlying the 'flattening of brain hierarchy' during movie-watching compared to rest and task states. This study supports the move toward naturalistic neuroscience paradigms, suggesting that engaging with complex stimuli like films reduces the steepness of hierarchical processing, potentially lowering thermodynamic costs associated with strict predictive coding.\n\nKringelbach, M.L. et al. (2023) published research in Science Advances on the mechanisms underlying the 'flattening of brain hierarchy' during movie-watching compared to rest and task states. This study supports the move toward naturalistic neuroscience paradigms, suggesting that engaging with complex stimuli like films reduces the steepness of hierarchical processing. This framework views naturalistic stimuli as a way to relax the brain's rigid predictive hierarchy, potentially offering a thermodynamic 'respite' or a different mode of efficient information processing compared to abstract tasks, thereby lowering thermodynamic costs associated with strict predictive coding.",
