      {
        "content": "Crisanti, A. and Ritort, F. (2003) discussed the 'Violation of the fluctuation–dissipation theorem in glassy systems' in Journal of Physics A. This citation suggests that the brain may exhibit 'glassy' dynamics, where the standard relationship between fluctuations and response is broken, indicating complex, non-equilibrium behavior that requires advanced statistical mechanical models to describe.\n\nCrisanti, A. and Ritort, F. (2003) discussed the 'Violation of the fluctuation–dissipation theorem in glassy systems' in Journal of Physics A. This citation suggests that the brain may exhibit 'glassy' dynamics, where the standard relationship between fluctuations and response is broken, indicating complex, non-equilibrium behavior that requires advanced statistical mechanical models to describe. Specifically, the concept of 'frustration' and slow dynamics is introduced into the brain model, implying operation near critical points similar to glasses. This can lead to the brain getting 'stuck' in local minima or exhibiting complex memory effects, which is relevant for understanding pathological states or memory consolidation.",
