      },
      {
        "content": "A recent fast whole-brain study utilizing magnetoencephalography (MEG) provided critical insight into how turbulence serves as the underlying skeleton for efficient spatiotemporal information transfer required for survival. This study links the physical properties of turbulence directly to the brain's ability to handle time-critical behavioral computations, suggesting that turbulence is a fundamental mechanism for survival-oriented processing.\n\nDeco, G. et al. (2023) investigated 'The effect of turbulence in brain dynamics information transfer measured with magnetoencephalography' in Communications Physics. This study provides empirical evidence linking turbulent brain dynamics to the efficiency of information transfer, using MEG to quantify how turbulent signatures facilitate or hinder communication between brain regions. It provides critical insight into how turbulence serves as the underlying skeleton for efficient spatiotemporal information transfer required for survival, linking the physical properties of turbulence directly to the brain's ability to handle time-critical behavioral computations.",
